\f0\fs24 \cf2 As soon as you enter Fausto\'92s apartment, you are greeted by a structure that looks like it belongs on a theatrical stage: it\'92s a 
\f1\i serliana
\f0\i0 . The name might sound technical, but the concept is simple: it\'92s a three-part opening with a central arch and two squared side openings. It was invented in the 16th century by Sebastiano Serlio, a hugely important architect who brought order to the language of the Renaissance.\
The 
\f1\i serliana
\f0\i0  you see here is truly special: enriched with two bronze roundels and directly inspired by the fa\'e7ade of Palazzo Sacrati Prosperi in Ferrara. The same model was also used for the portal of the Bagatti Valsecchi private residence on Via Santo Spirito 7. In short, a key reference for the brothers, which they reinterpreted in various parts of their home.\
But here\'92s the interesting part: the drawing you see in front of you does not represent this 
\f1\i serliana
\f0\i0  or the palace portal. And yet... It influenced them both! Several decorative motifs from the sketch were carried over exactly into the structure you\'92re looking at.\
Have you spotted them yet? Take a closer look: every detail is a clue to how an idea takes shape and transforms into architecture.\
